{"chunk_id": "09076e86542a", "text": "After withdrawing from his kingdom, Pandu lives in the mountains with Kunti and Madri, wandering through sacred Himalayan regions and eventually settling among ascetics. Through discipline and humility he earns the affection of Siddhas and Rishis, gaining the spiritual stature of a Brahmarshi despite being born a Kshatriya. When the sages prepare to journey northward to behold Brahman, they refuse to take Pandu and his wives because the path is too harsh for mortals. Pandu then reveals his anguish: he is sonless due to the curse he incurred when he slew a deer while it was mating. In Hindu thought, a man owes debts to gods, sages, ancestors, and humanity, and without children he cannot repay the debt to his ancestors. The sages foresee that he will indeed have divine-like offspring, prompting Pandu to confide in Kunti and seek a way to obtain sons despite his curse.\nPandu practices asceticism, seeks to join sages going to heaven, laments being sonless due to a curse, and asks Kunti to help obtain heirs.\nThemes: asceticism, ancestral duty, curse and infertility, quest for progeny, dharma and debts", "vector": [0.01942007802426815, -0.01797184906899929, 0.0043412428349256516, -0.0067101335152983665, -0.013675431720912457, 0.02768188714981079, -0.010958275757730007, -0.008061815053224564, -0.024799220263957977, -0.017820129171013832, 0.0037895359564572573, 0.006565310526639223, 0.018427006900310516, 0.03042662888765335, -0.0068825422786176205, -0.0007068742997944355, 0.03834361955523491, -0.018399421125650406, 0.034426502883434296, -0.013240962289273739, -0.026675023138523102, -0.011047928594052792, 0.004441239405423403, 0.0017930470639839768, -0.03555750101804733, 0.02255101501941681, 0.022357916459441185, -0.01579260639846325, 0.007234255317598581, -0.020068334415555, 0.0014189208159223199, 0.006241182796657085, -0.009854862466454506, -0.02001316286623478, -0.025599194690585136, -0.001149101764895022, -0.004772263579070568, -0.